>3 - What's the latest wisdom on whether attitudinals are significant to the
>logical meaning of a bridi?  If I say ".ianai le gerku cu barda", am I
>stating that the dog is big, but that the truth of that fills me with some
>kind of sense of disbelief -- or am I saying approximately "I don't believe
>the dog is big".

Standard wisdom is that attitudinals have no effect on the logical meaning of
a bridi, BUT they do affect whether the statement of the bridi is to be taken
as an assertion of truth.  I interpret .ianai as indicating a disbelief in
the truth of the bridi it is attached to - as to whether the claim is made

in spite of that disbelief, I would say that depends on context.  ("I don't
believe it but it is true").

Thus attitudinals have ENORMOUS pragmatic effect, but little logical effect.

